# Copy to .env.local and adjust. Everything here is NEXT_PUBLIC_*, which means
# it is inlined into the JavaScript bundle at build time and readable by anyone
# who loads the page. None of it is a secret, and nothing secret belongs here.
#
# Because these are baked in at build time rather than read at boot, changing
# any of them requires a rebuild — restarting the server is not enough.

# Where the API lives. The browser calls this directly, so it must be reachable
# from the user's machine, not just from the web server.
#
# In production this is cross-origin, so the API must list this app's origin in
# its own ALLOWED_ORIGINS or every request fails CORS. It must also be https
# when the site is served over https, or the browser blocks it as mixed content.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL="http://localhost:3001"

# --- Static export (GitHub Pages) -------------------------------------------
# Leave both unset for the normal deployment: a Next server, with the CSP nonce
# from src/proxy.ts and the security headers from next.config.ts.
#
# Setting NEXT_PUBLIC_STATIC_EXPORT=true produces a directory of files instead,
# for a host that runs no server code. That build cannot set response headers,
# so it loses X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy,
# Permissions-Policy and HSTS, and its CSP moves into a meta tag without a
# nonce. Use it only where a static host is the requirement.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STATIC_EXPORT=true

# Sub-path the site is served from, no trailing slash. A GitHub Pages project
# site lives at /<repo>. Leave unset for a root deployment or a custom domain.
# The Pages workflow sets this from the repository name.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_PATH="/GCOMS"
